<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 27 BCE – 235 CE (the Principate)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Emperor to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajans-column.jpg" alt="Trajan's Column"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column">Trajan's Column</a><span class="soc-credit">Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Roman society under the emperors was ranked by law as well as by wealth. Citizens of means were enrolled in formal orders (ordines): the senatorial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equites">equestrian orders</a> at the top and, in every town, the council of decurions. Each had a property qualification, a mark of dress and its own seats at the games. Below them lived the great mass of free people: farmers, craftsmen, traders and the urban poor. Most of them lived outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and, until 212 CE, were not Roman citizens. Freed slaves became citizens but carried the mark of their past. The enslaved, perhaps one person in eight, had no rights at all. A few thousand families held the honours of an empire of sixty to seventy-five million people.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 27 BCE – 235 CE (the Principate)</span><span><b>People</b> 59–76 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 8</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Princeps</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Ordo senatorius</span> <span class="soc-en">(Senators)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 10,000</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Equites</span> <span class="soc-en">(Equestrians)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.1–0.2%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Decuriones</span> <span class="soc-en">(Town councillors)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–1% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Milites</span> <span class="soc-en">(Soldiers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.3–0.6%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:83%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Plebs</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 83% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Liberti</span> <span class="soc-en">(Freed slaves)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:12%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Servi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">10–15%</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/trajan" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/trajan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Princeps</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">imperator, Augustus</p><p class="soc-kid">The emperor commanded the army, made the laws and had his face on the coins.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The emperor was in name the “first citizen” (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a>) and in fact the master of the state. He commanded the armies, controlled the richest provinces, chose the governors and generals, and was the final court of appeal. From Augustus onwards he held a bundle of the old republican powers rather than a crown, and no law fixed the succession: emperors adopted heirs, named their sons, or were made by the army. A household of slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> ran much of his government, and his portrait was on the coinage. After his death the Senate could declare a respected emperor a god (divus).</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> In law any man the Senate and the army accepted could rule. In practice emperors came from the senatorial order, and in the 3rd century more and more often from the army, as soldiers raised their own commanders to the throne.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the imperial family (the domus Augusta).</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus (Roman emperor)</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperator-romanorum">Imperator Romanorum</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/roman-senate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 10,000</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Ordo senatorius</span> <span class="soc-en">(Senators)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Senators were rich landowners who advised the emperor and governed the provinces.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The senatorial order stood highest. Augustus fixed the Senate at about 600 members and the property qualification at one million sesterces, and senators wore a broad purple stripe on the tunic. They governed most provinces, commanded the legions, held the old magistracies from quaestor to consul, and judged cases; as a body the Senate passed decrees and confirmed each new emperor. Their wealth lay in land, since open trade was thought beneath them. Twenty quaestors were chosen each year, which kept the Senate at its size. Many seats passed from father to son, but senatorial families died out fast, and the emperors filled the gaps with men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and, increasingly, from the provinces.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A rich equestrian entered when the emperor granted him the broad stripe or enrolled him among the former magistrates. A senator who fell below the property qualification, or offended the emperor, lost his seat.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 600 senators, the number Augustus fixed; with their families a few thousand people in an empire of 60–75 million.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pliny-the-elder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>0.1–0.2%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Equites</span> <span class="soc-en">(Equestrians)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">ordo equester</p><p class="soc-kid">Equestrians, or knights, were wealthy men who ran the emperor’s offices, money and some of his armies.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The equestrians, or knights, formed the second order. They needed property of 400,000 sesterces and wore a narrow purple stripe and a gold ring. Their name recalls the cavalry of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but under the emperors they were landowners, men of business and, above all, the emperor’s own officials. Equestrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and commanded the Praetorian Guard, procurators ran the imperial estates and taxes, and equestrian officers commanded auxiliary units. These posts were graded by salary, from 60,000 to 300,000 sesterces a year. Most equestrians never held office at all: they were the leading men of the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the provinces.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Sons of town councillors, veteran chief centurions and rich provincials entered the order by the emperor’s grant. The most successful equestrians, or their sons, could be raised to the Senate.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 25,000 men of equestrian rank in the 2nd century, a modern estimate; with their families roughly 0.1–0.2 per cent of the population.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equites">Equites</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.5–1% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Decuriones</span> <span class="soc-en">(Town councillors)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">ordo decurionum</p><p class="soc-kid">Every Roman town had a council of rich local men who ran its markets, temples and festivals.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Every Roman town had a council of decurions, drawn from the leading local families and bound by a property qualification that each town set for itself. The decurions chose the local magistrates, ran the markets, temples, water supply and festivals, and answered to the governor for the town’s taxes. They were expected to spend their own money on their town, and inscriptions across the empire record the baths, temples and games they paid for. By the 2nd and 3rd centuries the law counted them among the “more honourable” (honestiores), spared the beatings and degrading punishments it reserved for humbler people. From the 3rd century the duty of guaranteeing the taxes turned the office into a burden that many tried to escape.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Wealthy local men entered the council, often after holding a local magistracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> were generally barred, but their sons could enter.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: some 2,000 towns, each with a council of about 100 men; with their families perhaps 400,000–800,000 people among 60–75 million.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Marcus Holconius Rufus, five times chief magistrate of Pompeii: marble statue from Pompeii, 1st century CE · Sergey Sosnovskiy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuirassed_statue_of_Marcus_Olconius_Rufus_(51596875537).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auxilia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>0.3–0.6%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Milites</span> <span class="soc-en">(Soldiers)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Roman soldiers served for about 25 years and were given land or money when they retired.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The army was a professional force of long-serving volunteers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legionaries</a> had to be Roman citizens; they served for twenty years or more for regular pay, 225 denarii a year under Augustus, and received land or a cash bounty on discharge. The auxiliaries were recruited among provincials who were not citizens: they served twenty-five years and were granted citizenship at discharge, together with their children. Until about 197 CE serving soldiers could not legally marry. Soldiers enjoyed legal privileges, such as making a valid will without the usual formalities, and the centurions, the career officers, could rise to equestrian rank.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Enlisting gave a poor citizen a steady wage and a provincial a path to citizenship. A capable soldier could become a centurion and, as chief centurion, an equestrian.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 250,000 men under arms at the death of Augustus, and some 450,000 by 211 CE.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">Auxilia</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/latifundium" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/latifundium.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 83% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Plebs</span> <span class="soc-en">(Free people)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">peregrini (free <span class="soc-nw">non-citizens</span>), humiliores</p><p class="soc-kid">Most Romans were farmers, craftspeople and shopkeepers who worked hard to feed their families.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most of the empire’s free people were small farmers, tenants, labourers, craftsmen and shopkeepers. In the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> some 200,000 citizens received a free grain ration; elsewhere most people lived close to subsistence, and many worked land owned by others, as tenants (coloni) or labourers on the great estates. Until 212 CE most provincials were free non-citizens (peregrini) living under their own local law; that year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a> of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> made almost all free men citizens. By then the law divided the free into the “more honourable” and the “humbler” (humiliores), who could be flogged, sent to the mines or thrown to the beasts for crimes for which the honourable were exiled.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Wealth from trade, a soldier’s career or a patron’s favour could lift a family onto a town council within a generation or two. Debt and bad harvests pushed free farmers into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancy</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone else who was free: the great majority, most of them farmers. The town councillors and freed slaves are estimates, so this share is one too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plebeians">Plebeians</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latifundium">Latifundium</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 2–5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Liberti</span> <span class="soc-en">(Freed slaves)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">libertini</p><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people who were set free became Roman citizens, and their children were born free.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">A slave freed by a Roman citizen became a citizen, something Greek observers found remarkable, but not an equal one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> could not hold magistracies, sit on town councils or serve in the legions, and they owed their former owner, now their patron, respect, services and a share of their estate. Many carried on in the same trade or household. Some grew very rich: the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>’s freedmen secretaries Narcissus and Pallas ran the great offices of state, and wealthy freedmen across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> formed the priesthoods of the Augustales, which honoured the emperor. Their children were born free and carried none of these limits.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> were freeborn citizens and could enter the town councils, and their grandsons sometimes reached equestrian rank.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: freed slaves were numerous, above all in Rome and the towns of Italy, where funerary inscriptions name them in great numbers; far fewer in the countryside of the provinces.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Publius Aiedius Amphio and his wife Aiedia, freed slaves: funerary relief from the Via Appia, c. 30 BCE · Gary Todd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funerary_Relief_of_Publius_Aiedius_Amphio_and_his_Wife_Aiedia%2C_Marble%2C_Via_Appia%2C_Rome%2C_c._30_BC_(28117260474).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ancient-rome-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>10–15%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Servi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later set free.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Enslaved people were property. They could be bought, sold, beaten and separated from their families, and they could not legally marry. They came from war captives, the children of enslaved mothers, the trade across the frontiers and abandoned infants. Their lives varied greatly. Gangs on the great estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and the slaves and convicts in the mines, worked in brutal conditions; household slaves cooked, cleaned, nursed and served; skilled slaves worked as craftsmen, doctors, teachers, secretaries and managers, often allowed to keep savings (the peculium) with which they might buy their freedom. Freeing slaves was common, especially in the towns, and by the 2nd century most citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> probably had a slave somewhere among their ancestors.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came only by manumission: as a reward, by purchase with the slave’s own savings, or in the owner’s will. It made the freed slave a Roman citizen.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Modern estimates put the enslaved at about 10–15 per cent of the empire’s people: far more in Italy, far fewer in Egypt and North Africa.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Slaves serving wine at a banquet, mosaic from Dougga, 3rd century CE · Dennis G. Jarvis · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dougga_cup-bearers_mosa%C3%AFc.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="ancient-rome">Be born in the Roman Empire</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Roman society was steep but not closed. The orders were defined by wealth as well as birth, so a fortune made in trade, the army or the emperor’s service could carry a family up the ladder over two or three generations: from slave to freedman, from a freedman’s son to the town council, from a councillor’s son to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equites">equestrian order</a>, and from there to the Senate. The emperor controlled the top of the ladder, granting citizenship, equestrian rank and senatorial status, and he renewed the Senate with men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the provinces: the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> himself came from a family settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italica">Italica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Falls were just as real. Senators who lost their fortune or the emperor’s favour lost their rank, and free farmers sunk in debt became tenants, whom the laws of the 4th century tied to the land.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women took the rank of their fathers and husbands: the wives and daughters of senators shared their title. Women could not vote or hold office, and the law placed most of them under a guardian for important business, though Augustus freed freeborn women with three children, and freedwomen with four, from guardianship. Many women owned land and businesses, freed slaves and paid for public buildings: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> the priestess Eumachia built one of the largest buildings on the forum. Enslaved women had no protection from their owners, and their children were born enslaved.</p></section>
